$ so ws --help
Work in a directory of your own: a workspace holds a sparse copy of a
changeset's tree.

Edit its files with any tool, build and test there, then commit, rebase and land
with one command each. A command inside a workspace finds it and its store; --ws
NAME or --ws-path DIR names another.

Usage: so ws [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  create       Make a workspace in a directory of its own
  status       What changed in a workspace, and how far its target moved
  diff         A workspace's edits against its head
  commit       Record a workspace's edits as one changeset
  rebase       Move a workspace's chain onto its target's head
  resolve      Mark conflicting paths of a rebase resolved
  land         Land a workspace's chain on its target
  materialize  Write out more of a workspace's tree
  discard      Restore paths from the head: the edits are lost
  snapshot     Record the edits as a changeset, moving no ref
  list         List the store's workspaces
  info         Describe one workspace
  delete       Remove a workspace's directory, then its entry
  hook         Run by agent hosts: workspace hooks on stdin and stdout

Examples (SWARMOPS_REPO names the store, SWARMOPS_AGENT you):
  $ so ws create fix-1
  $ so --human ws status --ws fix-1

Learn more:
  A command's help  so ws <command> --help
  Guide             https://swarmops.cc/docs/guides/parallel-agents
  Reference         https://swarmops.cc/docs/reference/cli

Options:
      --repo <STORE>     The store to use (default: $SWARMOPS_REPO)
      --as <AGENT>       The agent to act as, <family>/<instance> (default:
                         $SWARMOPS_AGENT)
      --json             Print the JSON envelope: the default, unless
                         $SWARMOPS_FORMAT is human
      --human            Print a short text rendering instead of JSON
      --pretty[=<BOOL>]  Pretty-print the JSON (default: when stdout is a
                         terminal)
  -h, --help             Print help

$ so blob --help
Store content as a blob for a commit to name by its id, or ask which blobs the
store holds

Usage: so blob [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  put  Store a file's content as a blob, and print its id
  has  Of the ids given, list those the store holds
  get  Print the blobs the ids name

Examples (SWARMOPS_REPO names the store, SWARMOPS_AGENT you):
  $ so blob put logo.png

Learn more:
  A command's help  so blob <command> --help
  Guide             https://swarmops.cc/docs/guides/commit-and-land
  Reference         https://swarmops.cc/docs/reference/cli

Options:
      --repo <STORE>     The store to use (default: $SWARMOPS_REPO)
      --as <AGENT>       The agent to act as, <family>/<instance> (default:
                         $SWARMOPS_AGENT)
      --json             Print the JSON envelope: the default, unless
                         $SWARMOPS_FORMAT is human
      --human            Print a short text rendering instead of JSON
      --pretty[=<BOOL>]  Pretty-print the JSON (default: when stdout is a
                         terminal)
  -h, --help             Print help

$ so check --help
List the checks a tree names in .swarmops/checks.toml, run one stage of them,
read a log

Usage: so check [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  list  List the checks of a changeset's tree, or of a directory
  plan  Say which checks of one stage a change needs, and why
  run   Run one stage's checks of a clean workspace or a directory
  runs  List the run lines of a workspace's or a directory's checks
  log   Read a check's log of a run, or follow it as it grows

Examples:
  $ mkdir -p .swarmops
  $ echo 'version = 1' > .swarmops/checks.toml
  $ echo 'check.test.argv = ["true"]' >> .swarmops/checks.toml
  $ so --human check plan --dir . --changed README.md
  $ so --human check run --dir .

Learn more:
  A command's help  so check <command> --help
  Guide             https://swarmops.cc/docs/guides/parallel-agents
  Reference         https://swarmops.cc/docs/reference/cli

Options:
      --repo <STORE>     The store to use (default: $SWARMOPS_REPO)
      --as <AGENT>       The agent to act as, <family>/<instance> (default:
                         $SWARMOPS_AGENT)
      --json             Print the JSON envelope: the default, unless
                         $SWARMOPS_FORMAT is human
      --human            Print a short text rendering instead of JSON
      --pretty[=<BOOL>]  Pretty-print the JSON (default: when stdout is a
                         terminal)
  -h, --help             Print help

$ so ref --help
List refs, or move one by compare-and-swap

Usage: so ref [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  list    List refs, each with its kind
  set     Point a ref at a revision, by compare-and-swap
  delete  Delete a ref
  log     List a ref's moves, newest first
  txn     Move several refs as one operation: all or none

Examples (SWARMOPS_REPO names the store, SWARMOPS_AGENT you):
  $ so --human ref list
  $ so ref set release/1.0 main --create

Learn more:
  A command's help  so ref <command> --help
  Guide             https://swarmops.cc/docs/guides/history-and-undo
  Reference         https://swarmops.cc/docs/reference/cli

Options:
      --repo <STORE>     The store to use (default: $SWARMOPS_REPO)
      --as <AGENT>       The agent to act as, <family>/<instance> (default:
                         $SWARMOPS_AGENT)
      --json             Print the JSON envelope: the default, unless
                         $SWARMOPS_FORMAT is human
      --human            Print a short text rendering instead of JSON
      --pretty[=<BOOL>]  Pretty-print the JSON (default: when stdout is a
                         terminal)
  -h, --help             Print help

$ so op --help
List the store's operations, or undo one

Usage: so op [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  log   List the store's operations, newest first
  undo  Move every ref an operation moved back, all or none

Examples (SWARMOPS_REPO names the store, SWARMOPS_AGENT you):
  $ so --human op log

